WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:08.340
Sanitizers work best when they are part of a normal developer workflow rather than a special event after a problem reaches production.

2
00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:13.800
Local sanitizer builds help engineers reproduce findings quickly while a change is still fresh.

3
00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:18.300
Pull-request checks can catch regressions before merge.

4
00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:24.400
Scheduled CI jobs can run broader or slower sanitizer suites without blocking every small edit.

5
00:00:24.680 --> 00:00:26.360
Test selection matters.

6
00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:29.580
Unit tests are useful for focused boundary behavior.

7
00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:34.560
Integration tests exercise ownership and lifetime across component seams.

8
00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:40.260
Longer-running tests can reveal shutdown, cancellation, and cleanup problems.

9
00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:49.160
Defensive fuzzing can be useful at a high level for parsers and data-heavy interfaces when it is run in a controlled environment and findings are handled responsibly.

10
00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:55.820
The goal is meaningful execution, because sanitizers can only report what the instrumented run observes.

11
00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:58.100
Teams also need triage ownership.

12
00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:08.920
A sanitizer finding should be tracked as an engineering defect, assigned, reproduced, investigated, and fixed or explicitly deferred with clear reasoning.

13
00:01:09.020 --> 00:01:11.900
Permanent suppression of real issues should be avoided.

14
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:20.960
If a temporary suppression is necessary, keep it specific, documented, reviewed, and tied to a known limitation or third-party dependency.

15
00:01:21.240 --> 00:01:23.240
Practical workflow is a balance.

16
00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:29.420
Sanitizer jobs should run often enough to catch regressions early, but not in a way that developers learn to ignore.

17
00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:40.480
Keep reports actionable, keep configurations reproducible, and make sanitizer failures part of the same quality conversation as tests, code review, and static analysis.

18
00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:43.160
Good teams also watch the age of findings.

19
00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:49.900
A sanitizer dashboard or issue queue that only grows teaches developers that the signal is optional.

20
00:01:50.000 --> 00:01:57.520
Closing reports, removing obsolete suppressions, and periodically reviewing skipped tests keeps the workflow credible.

21
00:01:57.620 --> 00:02:03.140
The goal is a system where a new sanitizer failure feels actionable, not like background noise.
